<InsightArticle title="A cheap first pass stops saving money when too much work goes round twice" description="A two-stage AI workflow has a calculable break-even point. Frozen AIRH rates show how escalation changes the bill before any quality benefit is claimed." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="9 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="Using two frozen AIRH price rows, a hypothetical first pass costs $21.50 for 10,000 jobs and the direct alternative costs $31.50. Sending half the queue through both raises the model-token bill to $37.25, above the direct route.">
<p>Cheap-first routing sounds economical because the first call is cheaper. The total also contains the calls made afterwards. In this particular example, that second amount uses up the saving when more than about 31.75% of jobs need the alternative. That is a break-even calculation, meaning the point where two cost plans become equal, and not a finding about either model's quality.</p>
<p>Routing here means choosing which model handles a job. Escalation means sending a job to the second model after its first pass. Those definitions do not imply that the second model is better. We have not tested either model on these tasks, and the labels describe the application's sequence rather than a quality hierarchy.</p>
<h2>Why the first-pass bill never disappears</h2>
<p>The first stage uses the frozen Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite row at $0.30 per million input tokens and $2.50 per million output tokens. The alternative uses the Mistral Large 4 row at $0.70 and $2.10. These records were read from AIRH's 14 November 2026 snapshot for this guide on the same date. They are preserved catalogue rates, not fresh vendor quotes.</p>
<p>Each hypothetical stage receives 3,000 input tokens and produces 500 output tokens. That gives $0.00215 per first-stage job and $0.00315 per alternative job. Every original job runs through the first stage. Some then run through the alternative as well. An escalation adds a full second call under the example's assumptions; it does not refund the first one.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={['Share escalated','First stage: dollars','Second stage: dollars','Total dollars']} rows={d.routing.map(r=>[`${r.fallbackShare*100}%`,r.firstStage,r.secondStage,r.total])} caption="10,000 hypothetical jobs, identical 3,000-input/500-output counts at each stage. Escalation shares are invented, not pass rates. Direct use of the alternative costs $31.50 under the same counts. No judging, tools, retries or human review charges are included." />
<p>The quarter-escalation row totals $29.375 before money is rounded for display. It sits below the direct route's $31.50. The half-escalation row totals $37.25 and sits above it. An application can therefore spend more by starting with the cheaper call, even when its second call costs exactly what the direct alternative would have cost.</p>
<p>If all jobs escalate, the total is $53: both stage bills added together. If none escalates, it is $21.50. Those endpoints are properties of this invented fixed-count workflow. They do not describe the lowest or highest bill a real application could produce, where different prompts, repeated attempts and variable output lengths may change both stage costs.</p>
<h2>Where the 31.75% boundary comes from</h2>
<p>Let C1 be the cost per first-stage job, C2 the cost per alternative job, and f the fraction escalated. The expected cost under this fixed-price setup is C1 plus f multiplied by C2. Direct use costs C2. Equating those expressions gives f = 1 minus C1 divided by C2. Inserting the frozen-rate calculations gives about 0.31746, or 31.75% when rounded.</p>
<p>This expression assumes that the alternative costs the same in both plans. If escalation adds the first answer, a critique or extra evidence to the second prompt, that assumption stops holding. The second stage then needs its own input and output measurement. The general comparison remains first-stage cost plus the escalated share multiplied by escalation cost, compared with direct cost.</p>
<p>A separate automatic judge would add another term. A judge is a procedure, possibly another model, used to decide whether an answer meets the task's written requirements. The table assumes none is charged. A team considering paid judging would need to include those calls even on jobs that never escalate. Otherwise the projected saving omits part of the proposed workflow.</p>
<p>The boundary is also sensitive to the task. With enough output, these two recorded models reverse their price order, as our <a href="/blog/blended-price-ranking-reversal/">guide to blended prices</a> shows. Cheap-first is therefore a description of this workload, not a permanent property of the model names. A routing rule should identify the task and counts that made its first stage cheaper.</p>
<h2>Who decides that another pass is needed?</h2>
<p>The escalated share alone cannot establish whether the router is useful. A router could achieve a low share by accepting incorrect answers. It could achieve a high share by rejecting answers that already meet the requirement. Both affect the bill, but they create different kinds of work for whoever uses the results.</p>
<p>A meaningful trial needs reviewed outcomes for jobs accepted at the first stage and jobs sent onwards. Reviewing only the escalated cases leaves the accepted group unchecked. The number of missed errors in that group is part of the evidence needed before treating a low escalation rate as a success. This guide provides no measurement of that number.</p>
<p>The second-stage answer needs checking as well. Escalation is an action, not proof of a correction. If the alternative also fails, the original job remains unresolved. A cost report that counts a second response as a completed job without examining it would make the workflow look more productive than the evidence supports.</p>
<p>For comparison, both the direct route and the two-stage route need the same written acceptance rule and a comparable task set. The rule defines what counts as usable. A cheaper route with a different standard is answering a different purchasing question. The arithmetic can price the routes, but it cannot choose the standard on behalf of the people relying on them.</p>
<h2>A financial boundary is narrower than an operating decision</h2>
<p>Latency, meaning elapsed time before the usable result arrives, is absent from this table. The guide does not infer it from model names or prices. A second pass may add time, but its actual effect on a queue needs measurement in the application. Someone choosing a route may accept a higher bill for a time or quality benefit that has been separately demonstrated.</p>
<p>The cost of reviewing and correcting errors is absent too. A realistic business comparison can include that work, with measured review times and a stated costing method. Adding a guessed hourly charge would make the table more elaborate without improving its evidence. Here the deliberately narrow question is whether the model-token charges alone leave room for a saving.</p>
<p>The frozen file retains source labels and their import timestamp. Neither turns this into a current offer or a verified quality comparison. The <a href="/transparency/#workload-price-guides">transparency receipt</a> records the original file, its hash, formulas and omissions. No Artificial Analysis or Arena figures enter the calculation, and no provider requests were made to obtain the imagined escalation rates.</p>
<p>A useful results log for a real trial would keep original jobs distinct from calls. It would show which route each job took, whether the final answer passed, and the charges attached to each stage. That lets a reviewer calculate both the actual escalated share and the actual cost without mistaking two calls for two completed jobs.</p>
<p>The example's boundary gives the trial a specific economic question. At the stated counts and rates, an escalation share above about 31.75% removes the model-token saving. Evidence of better outcomes might still justify the route. A claim that it saves money would need a different measured bill or different explicitly stated assumptions.</p>
<p>There is also a difference between a routing decision made before generation and one made afterwards. A rule that sends a known category directly to the alternative avoids paying for a first-stage response on those jobs. The table assumes the opposite: the first stage runs for the entire queue. A proposal for category-based routing needs its own formula and evidence that the category can be identified at that point.</p>
<p>That evidence should include cases routed incorrectly. Sending a hard case to the first model may still leave it unresolved; sending an easy case directly to the alternative may consume a saving that was available. Neither error rate is supplied by the catalogue. Keeping those unknowns beside the break-even result prevents a precise financial threshold from sounding like a validated operating policy.</p>
</InsightArticle>
